  {
    "version": "1.4.8",
    "date": "2026-09-04",
    "summary": "Every camera tile is a square that centres on your face, and a project\nfinally remembers what happened on it."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.4.7",
    "date": "2026-09-04",
    "summary": "Team folds into Work, and Work grows a menu, filters, and a right-click."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.4.6",
    "date": "2026-09-04",
    "summary": "Issues you can finish without opening GitHub."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.4.5",
    "date": "2026-09-04",
    "summary": "The Work window grew a calendar, a third column, and issues that read like\nGitHub's own."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.4.4",
    "date": "2026-09-03"
  },
  {
    "version": "1.4.3",
    "date": "2026-09-03",
    "summary": "The work that had been sitting uncommitted on the shared Mac is in, and the\nWork window learned how a Mac window is shaped."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.4.2",
    "date": "2026-09-02",
    "summary": "The calendar and the work now know each other."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.4.1",
    "date": "2026-09-02",
    "summary": "A project's channel now carries the meeting's trail."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.4.0",
    "date": "2026-09-02",
    "summary": "A meeting and the project it is about now know each other."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.3.9",
    "date": "2026-09-02",
    "summary": "What is yours can be finished where it is read."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.3.8",
    "date": "2026-09-02",
    "summary": "Home knows what you owe today."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.3.7",
    "date": "2026-09-02",
    "summary": "Work gets its door on the Mac, and a message on a project finds its way there."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.3.6",
    "date": "2026-09-02"
  },
  {
    "version": "1.3.5",
    "date": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
    "version": "1.3.4",
    "date": "2026-09-01",
    "summary": "Suggested tasks got a better ear, ticking one off got simpler, and the lime\nbutton stopped shouting on a white page."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.3.3",
    "date": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
    "version": "1.3.2",
    "date": "2026-09-01"
  },
  {
    "version": "1.3.1",
    "date": "2026-08-28",
    "summary": "Five fixes from a top-to-bottom review, all in the paths that looked healthy\nwhile they weren't."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.3.0",
    "date": "2026-08-24",
    "summary": "Work is where the projects and leads live, and it now knows about GitHub and\nabout you."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.2.2",
    "date": "2026-08-23",
    "summary": "Three things that did not work in the last release, and one that was only ugly."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.2.1",
    "date": "2026-08-23",
    "summary": "Two things you notice on a phone: being asked who you are, and being told\nsomething the app has not checked yet."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.2.0",
    "date": "2026-08-22",
    "summary": "The iPhone can host. Not a remote control for the Mac — the same job: it starts\nthe room, answers the door, transcribes on the device, and files the meeting."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.1.0",
    "date": "2026-08-20",
    "summary": "Meet has only ever had a dark appearance. It has a light one now, and a setting\nto choose between them — which is why this is 1.1.0 and not another patch."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.0.8",
    "date": "2026-08-20",
    "summary": "The Calendar sidebar had grown into a list of two different things: switches for\nwhat you can see today, and chores like connecting an account. The chores have\nmoved to Settings, and the company rows are one short list."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.0.7",
    "date": "2026-08-19",
    "summary": "At seven in the evening the app would put tomorrow's first meeting in the\ncountdown card and tick down fourteen hours to it, as though it were about to\nstart. A day that is over should say so."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.0.6",
    "date": "2026-08-19",
    "summary": "The iPhone calendar could not be scrolled reliably, and half the screen was\nchrome rather than day."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.0.5",
    "date": "2026-08-19",
    "summary": "Two meetings at the same hour used to make each other unreadable, and a week\nmeant something different depending on which screen you read it on."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.0.4",
    "date": "2026-08-19",
    "summary": "Meet never records — but the Mac kept the camera open anyway, for a preview\nthat was behind another screen."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.0.3",
    "date": "2026-08-19",
    "summary": "Meet has no push. A direct message is only ever found by opening the app — and\nthe screen you open first said nothing about it."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.0.2",
    "date": "2026-08-19",
    "summary": "Reaching a colleague stops being a procedure. Call is one click, your own door\nis watched while you are out, and a knock is something you actually hear."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.0.1",
    "date": "2026-08-19",
    "summary": "Team stops being a bulletin board. It already said who was signed in; now it\nsays whether you can interrupt them, where to find them, and gives you a way\nto reach them that is not a meeting invitation."
  },
  {
    "version": "1.0.0",
    "date": "2026-08-19",
    "summary": "The version goes 0.8.0 → 1.0.0. Everything numbered 0.9.x was bumped but never\nshipped — no tag was ever cut and the update feed kept offering 0.8.0 — while\nthe release grew underneath those numbers into something that is not a patch:\na people surface, private messaging with its own storage and jurisdiction, and\na real calendar on both native apps.\n\nThe rolling rule reserves 1.0.0 for after 0.9.9, so this is a deliberate\nexception rather than a slip. It also matters mechanically: the release\nworkflow only builds iOS to TestFlight from a tag whose version ends in `.0`,\nso a `0.9.x` tag would have shipped the Mac and left every iPhone on 0.8.0."
  },
  {
    "version": "0.8.0",
    "date": "2026-08-19"
